Step 10 - Install the CSIDriver


What is a CSIDriver?

This is not a Pod.

It is a Kubernetes object that tells Kubernetes:

"There is a CSI driver called rbd.csi.ceph.com."

Without it, Kubernetes doesn't know this storage driver exists.

Think of it like registering a new storage driver with the Kubernetes API.


root@kube-master:~/ceph-csi/deploy/rbd/kubernetes# kubectl apply -f csidriver.yaml
csidriver.storage.k8s.io/rbd.csi.ceph.com created

root@kube-master:~/ceph-csi/deploy/rbd/kubernetes# kubectl get csidriver
NAME               ATTACHREQUIRED   PODINFOONMOUNT   STORAGECAPACITY   TOKENREQUESTS   REQUIRESREPUBLISH   MODES        AGE
rbd.csi.ceph.com   true             false            false             <unset>         false               Persistent   22s



Step 11 - Install RBAC
Why do we need RBAC?

The CSI driver needs permission to:

Watch PVCs
Create PVs
Update PVC status
Watch Nodes
Create Events
Read Secrets
Read ConfigMaps

Without RBAC, the CSI pods will start but continuously get "Forbidden" errors.


Why are there two RBAC files?

Because there are two different CSI components.

1. Provisioner RBAC
PVC
 │
 ▼
Provisioner
 │
Creates RBD Image
 │
Creates PV

It needs permissions to work with:

PVC
PV
StorageClass
Secrets


2. Node Plugin RBAC
Worker Node
      │
Node Plugin
      │
Mount RBD
      │
Pod

It needs permissions related to:

Nodes
Pods
VolumeAttachment
Secrets


